{
  "slug": "ffel-loans-explained",
  "title": "How to Check for FFEL Loans and Make Them PSLF-Eligible",
  "metaDescription": "How to tell if you have Federal Family Education Loan (FFEL) loans, and what consolidating them for Public Service Loan Forgiveness costs you.",
  "h1": "How to Find Your FFEL Loans and Make Them Count for PSLF",
  "cardBlurb": "Spot FFEL loans on StudentAid.gov and weigh what consolidating them for PSLF costs you.",
  "introText": "You have FFEL loans if any loan under View Loans on [StudentAid.gov](https://studentaid.gov/articles/what-to-know-about-ffel-loans/) shows \"FFEL\" at the front of its listing, which marks it as a Federal Family Education Loan (FFEL) Program loan. FFEL loans do not count toward Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) unless you consolidate them into a Direct Consolidation Loan. At The Modern Wallet, we build every student loan guide around one decision. For FFEL borrowers, that decision is whether consolidating for PSLF is worth the payment credit and rate reduction it can cost.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "How to Tell If You Have FFEL Loans",
      "body": "The quickest check for FFEL loans is the loan list on StudentAid.gov. Log in, find Loan Breakdown, and select View Loans. Any loan with \"FFEL\" at the front of its listing is an FFEL Program loan.\n\nThe My Aid section gives a second view, showing whether each loan is a Direct Loan or a commercial FFELP loan. FFELP is short for Federal Family Education Loan Program, the same program under its full name. The National Student Loan Data System (NSLDS) is another place that shows which kind of loan you have.\n\nThe FFEL Program stopped making new loans on July 1, 2010. If you were in school before that date, you may still have an FFEL loan. For how FFEL loans sit next to Direct Loans and private loans, see our [types of student loans guide](/guides/types-of-student-loans/)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "FFEL Loans and PSLF Eligibility",
      "body": "FFEL loans do not qualify for PSLF in their current form, because only Direct Loans count toward the program. Perkins loans and private loans do not qualify either. [StudentAid.gov's FFEL explainer](https://studentaid.gov/articles/what-to-know-about-ffel-loans/) states that FFEL and Perkins loans may become eligible if they are consolidated into a Direct Consolidation Loan.\n\nThat makes consolidation the route from an FFEL loan to PSLF. The cost is that consolidating generally means losing credit for earlier payments. Since only Direct Loans count toward PSLF, payments on the FFEL loan itself were never PSLF payments.\n\nForgiveness is not automatic after you consolidate. You still have to meet the requirements listed at [StudentAid.gov/publicservice](https://studentaid.gov/publicservice), and new PSLF regulations take effect on July 1, 2026. If forgiveness does come through, read [whether student loan forgiveness is taxable](/guides/is-student-loan-forgiveness-taxable/) before you plan around the amount."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who Holds an FFEL Loan",
      "body": "The servicer name on the StudentAid.gov dashboard shows who holds an FFEL loan. Open My Loan Servicers and read the name listed for each loan. If the name starts with \"ED,\" the loan is held by the U.S. Department of Education (ED).\n\nIf the servicer name does not start with \"ED,\" the loan is a commercially held FFEL loan. The holder does not change the PSLF answer. An ED-held FFEL loan and a commercial FFEL loan are both outside the Direct Loan program, so neither counts toward PSLF until it is consolidated into a Direct Consolidation Loan.\n\nNote each servicer name before you start any application. That way you can match every loan on your list to the company that services it."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Three Costs of a Direct Consolidation Loan",
      "body": "A Direct Consolidation Loan can make an FFEL loan PSLF-eligible, and [StudentAid.gov's consolidation checklist](https://studentaid.gov/articles/5-things-before-consolidating-student-loans/) lists what it can cost you in return.\n\n- Payment credit. Consolidating generally means losing credit for earlier payments.\n- On-time rate reduction. Many FFEL borrowers get a reduced interest rate for paying on time, and adding an FFEL loan to a Direct Consolidation Loan can cost you that reduction.\n- Repayment length. Consolidation can lengthen repayment, so you may pay over more years.\n\nYou can see the repayment change before you commit. Steps 1 and 2 of the Direct Consolidation Loan Application show how consolidation will change your monthly payment and total repayment period. To check those figures against your own math, use our [guide to calculating your monthly student loan payment](/guides/how-to-calculate-your-monthly-student-loan-payment/)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "A Decision Rule for Consolidating",
      "body": "Consolidate an FFEL loan for PSLF only if you work for a qualifying employer, and only after you compare Steps 1 and 2 of the application. PSLF is the reason to consolidate here. Without a qualifying employer, you take on the three costs above and get no PSLF benefit in return.\n\nThis rule does not fit you if you have no plan to pursue PSLF and you get an on-time rate reduction on your FFEL loan. Keeping the loan as it is protects that reduction. A private refinance is no shortcut to PSLF either, because private loans do not qualify, so read [our guide on whether to refinance student loans](/guides/should-you-refinance-student-loans/) before you go that way.\n\nOur answer would change if the requirements look different once the final PSLF regulations take effect on July 1, 2026. Reread [StudentAid.gov/publicservice](https://studentaid.gov/publicservice) before you file, and redo the comparison if the employer or loan rules there have moved."
    },
    {
      "heading": "The Passed IDR Deadline and the 2026 PSLF Rules",
      "body": "The deadline to consolidate for the one-time income-driven repayment (IDR) account adjustment was June 30, 2024, and it has passed. Borrowers had to consolidate by that date to benefit from the adjustment, so consolidating today does not bring it back. Any advice urging FFEL borrowers to consolidate fast for the IDR adjustment is out of date.\n\nThe PSLF rules are changing too. The Education Department published final PSLF regulations on Oct. 30, 2025, and they take effect July 1, 2026. Read the current requirements at [StudentAid.gov/publicservice](https://studentaid.gov/publicservice) before you rely on any summary of them.\n\nThe [Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB)](https://www.consumerfinance.gov/paying-for-college/student-loan-forgiveness/) publishes its own student loan forgiveness page. Use it as a second government source alongside StudentAid.gov."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What to Do First",
      "body": "Start with the loan list, then the job, then the numbers.\n\n1. Log in to StudentAid.gov, select View Loans under Loan Breakdown, and mark every listing that starts with \"FFEL.\"\n2. Open My Loan Servicers and note whether each servicer name starts with \"ED.\"\n3. Confirm that your employer qualifies by reading the current rules at [StudentAid.gov/publicservice](https://studentaid.gov/publicservice).\n4. Fill in Steps 1 and 2 of the Direct Consolidation Loan Application and compare the new monthly payment and repayment period with your current ones.\n5. Decide whether PSLF is worth the earlier payment credit and any on-time rate reduction you would give up.\n\nIf you also hold newer federal loans, our [subsidized vs. unsubsidized comparison](/compare/subsidized-vs-unsubsidized-student-loans/) and [borrowing limits guide](/guides/student-loan-borrowing-limits/) explain those loan types. Your first move today is to log in to StudentAid.gov and open View Loans to see whether any of your loans are FFEL loans."
    }
  ],
