  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "How do I know if I have an FFEL loan?",
      "answer": "Log in to StudentAid.gov, go to Loan Breakdown, and select View Loans. Any loan with \"FFEL\" at the front of its listing is an FFEL Program loan. The My Aid section also shows whether each loan is a Direct Loan or a commercial FFELP loan, and the National Student Loan Data System (NSLDS) is another place that shows it."
    },
    {
      "question": "Are FFEL loans eligible for PSLF?",
      "answer": "No, not in their current form, because only Direct Loans count toward Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF). FFEL loans may become eligible if you consolidate them into a Direct Consolidation Loan. Consolidating generally means losing credit for earlier payments."
    },
    {
      "question": "Are FFEL loans eligible for forgiveness?",
      "answer": "For PSLF, FFEL loans become eligible only after you consolidate them into a Direct Consolidation Loan, because only Direct Loans count. Forgiveness is not automatic even then, since you still have to meet the requirements at [StudentAid.gov/publicservice](https://studentaid.gov/publicservice). The [CFPB's student loan forgiveness page](https://www.consumerfinance.gov/paying-for-college/student-loan-forgiveness/) is a second government source on forgiveness."
    },
    {
      "question": "Why are FFEL loans not eligible for PSLF?",
      "answer": "FFEL loans are not eligible because PSLF counts only Direct Loans, and FFEL loans come from a separate program that ended for new loans on July 1, 2010. Consolidating an FFEL loan into a Direct Consolidation Loan is how it may become eligible."
    },
    {
      "question": "What are FFEL loans?",
      "answer": "FFEL loans are federal student loans made under the Federal Family Education Loan (FFEL) Program, which stopped making new loans on July 1, 2010. Some are held by the U.S. Department of Education and show a servicer name starting with \"ED\" on StudentAid.gov. Others are commercially held FFELP loans."
    },
    {
      "question": "How to get FFEL loans forgiven?",
      "answer": "To pursue PSLF, consolidate your FFEL loans into a Direct Consolidation Loan while you work for a qualifying employer, then meet the requirements at [StudentAid.gov/publicservice](https://studentaid.gov/publicservice). Compare Steps 1 and 2 of the consolidation application first, because consolidating can lengthen repayment and cost you an on-time rate reduction. Forgiveness is not automatic."
    }
  ],
